tor. The Generate instruction should be to extract all numerical values. Then, the Revise instruction would process those numbers to associate them with the years. Then, the Ensemble would compare the two.\n\nAnother possible step is to use Summarize to condense the key information, but maybe that\'s not necessary here.\n\nSo the code would look like:\n\n1. Generate all numbers.\n2. Revise to associate numbers with years.\n3. Ensemble to compare.\n\nBut the user\'s example shows that the answer is 1997 had more. So the workflow should correctly extract 400k and 70k, then compare them.\n\nNow, translating this into the Python code. The run_workflow method needs to use the operators in sequence. The first step is to generate the numbers, then revise to get the correct associations, then ensemble to compare.\n\nThe instructions for Generate would be something like "Extract all numerical values from the passage, including percentages and counts." The context is the problem_text.\n\nThen, the Revise instruction would be to "Identify which numbers correspond to the years 1997 and 2008, and associate them with the correct years." The context would be the generated numbers.\n\nThen, the Ensemble instruction would be to "Compare the employment numbers for 1997 and 2008 and determine which year had more people employed." The contexts would be the revised numbers.\n\nBut the code needs to handle the asynchronous calls. So using asyncio.gather for parallel steps if needed, but in this case, it\'s sequential.\n\nSo the code would be:\n\nasync def run_workflow(self):\n    import asyncio\n    # Step 1: Extract all numerical values\n    numerical_values = await self.generate(\n        instruction="Extract all numerical values from the passage, including percentages and counts.",\n        context=self.problem_text\n    )\n    \n    # Step 2: Revise to associate numbers with years\n    revised_values = await self.revise(\n        instruction="Identify which numbers correspond to the 